# Git

A question is not permission. `reset`, `rebase`, `push --force`, `push --force-with-lease`, `branch -D`, `clean`, `checkout -- <path>`, and `worktree remove --force` run only on an order that names the operation and the target SHA or ref. That holds for the agent's own uncommitted edit too: undo it with an edit. In T6 the agent ran `git checkout --` on a test file it had just changed, with no order (PR #192). A worktree with changes is removed after its changes are undone with an edit or committed, so that `git worktree remove` needs no `--force`; in T8 the agent forced the removal of a prototype worktree with no order (PR #198), and in T1a it forced the removal of a worktree made for a comparison, whose only change was a copied benchmark file (PR #285). Before removing any worktree, run `git -C <worktree> status --short`; undo what it lists, then remove it without `--force`. Run the removal from outside the worktree: with the shell's directory inside it, git unregistered the worktree and left its folder (`Permission denied`, twice in T8); check that a folder left behind is empty before removing it. A script that undoes an edit searches the end of the range after its start: in T8 an undo took an earlier copy of the end line and doubled 280 lines of a file, which `git diff` showed (PR #198's retrospective).

This holds for a conflict that another PR's merge caused: ask how to solve it (rebase and force-push, or merge `main` into the branch) before running either. In L7 the agent rebased the branch on `main` and reset another branch without an order after PR #136 merged; the owner then ordered the force pushes (PR #138).

When asked about such an operation, answer with the current `HEAD` SHA, the target SHA, what would disappear, and the exact command. Then wait.

- Finished work goes to a PR in the same turn, without waiting for an ask: commit on the Issue's branch, push it, and open the PR (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`). The owner reviews on the PR, from wherever they are; uncommitted changes on this machine cannot be reviewed. Do not stop at "may I commit?".
- Long work runs in an agent worktree (`git worktree add .dev/<branch> <branch>`), not in the main working tree: the owner may switch that tree to another branch to review. In L2 the tree moved to `main` during the work; two edits landed there uncommitted, and the comparison apps were built from `main` (PR #125).
- A conflict resolved in a code file is built before the merge is committed. PR #570 took both sides of a test file's conflict with a script; the join dropped a closing brace, and the gate after the merge's commit found it.
- A conflict resolved in a document by a script is read back before the merge is committed: the file is not empty, holds no conflict marker, and `git diff --stat` against each parent shows the lines expected from that side. In PR #871 a script resolved `docs/conventions.md` to an empty file; the merge deleted all 94 lines, the gate checks no document, and the file stayed empty on `main` through fourteen merges until a draft that had to add a row to it found it. The script and `git commit` run in one `&&` chain, so that a script that stops also stops the commit: on the TH1 branch (PR #929) the resolving script raised on a `print`, the commit came after a `;`, and a merge with conflict markers in `docs/roadmap.md` was committed. It was found before the push because the file was read back.
- Before a worktree is removed, the processes working in its folder are stopped: a Monitor or a background shell that `cd`s into it, and the build servers (`dotnet build-server shutdown`). In T13 and D3, `git worktree remove` failed with "Permission denied" while a Monitor watching the CI ran in the folder; the worktree was unregistered and its folder left behind (PR #438, PR #442). A background command started while the session's directory was in the worktree is stopped too, even when the command `cd`s elsewhere: in P02a a CI watcher started that way kept the folder busy ("Device or resource busy"), and the folder was removed only after the watcher was stopped (PR #461). The shell's own directory counts too: in P02e `git worktree remove` ran while the shell was in the worktree, failed with "Permission denied", and left the folder empty and unregistered (`.dev/m614`, PR #615).
- This covers the Issue's own branch only. Never commit to `main` or push to it, and never merge.
- Who merged a PR, and when, is read from `gh pr view <n> --json mergedBy,mergedAt` and compared with the time of the agent's own merge command before it is reported. PR #574 had an order to merge after the checks; the owner merged it at 09:47 while the agent's watcher still waited on the CI, and the agent first reported that its watcher had merged it.
- `git branch --show-current` is part of the command that commits. A chain that creates a branch can fail before it does, and the commit then lands on `main`.
- A push that follows a test is chained to it so that a failed test stops the push (`./build.ps1 -Affected -Parallel; if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) { git push }`). The gate checks what `build.ps1` runs: the format of the C# files (CSharpier), the native libraries, the build, and the tests. It reads no file under `docs/` and no rule or skill, so a change to those files alone is pushed without the gate, and an edit of them after the gate has passed does not need it again; an edit of anything the gate reads does. CI follows the same line: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` starts no run for a change under `docs/`, `.cursor/`, `.agents/`, `.claude/`, or to a Markdown file at the root alone (Issue #802), so a PR of documents shows no checks, and that is not a failure to start. Before running a check or waiting on one, say what it verifies; if it verifies nothing the change touched, do not run it and do not wait. In D15 and D16 the agent ran the gate, about 20 minutes each time, for five pushes of design documents and rules, and held a merge behind CI, and the owner asked what the gate was verifying (PR #801). An earlier form of this rule asked for the gate again after any edit, documents included (PR #772). When the chain runs in the background, the PR's body is written before it starts and `gh pr create` is part of the chain: the session can end while the gate runs. Row Q12's branch was pushed by such a chain after the session had stopped, and stayed on the remote with no PR until the owner said the work had stopped (PR #777's retrospective). One local gate runs at a time: the gate of another PR starts when the one before it ended. In IM1 3c three gates ran at once, the tests of `XylemApp.Run` ran into their 5 s watchdog (Issue #793), and the gate of PR #794 was run three more times (PR #794's retrospective).
- When the gate fails only on a test that an open Issue tracks and the PR does not touch, the push still stops. The gate stops at the first failing step, so run the remaining test projects by hand; then write the failed test and its Issue under Verification and ask the owner whether to push. In PR #130 (only `docs/`) the gate failed on #126's message-loop test, the rest passed by hand, and the owner allowed the push.
- Read the CI of a pushed head through its Windows job before the next push to the branch: a push cancels the run in progress, and that head's result stays unknown. In V3c the first head's Windows run was cancelled by the next push, and the test that hung `XylemUI.Desktop.Tests` for 27 minutes was found one run later (PR #381).
- Read `gh pr view <n> --json state` before pushing to a branch that has a PR. A push to the branch of a merged PR recreates the deleted branch and reaches nothing.
- Fetch before pushing to a branch that has a PR, and compare it with the local branch (`git status -sb`). The owner may rebase a stacked branch after its base merges: in PR #327 the local branch was 5 ahead and 6 behind. Continue on the remote head (`git switch --detach origin/<branch>`) and push a fast-forward; do not push the stale local branch.
- Before pushing a fix for a review, check that the PR is still open (`gh pr view <n> --json state`). Once it is merged, the fix goes on the next PR's branch: in PR #486 a push after the merge made the branch again on the remote, and its commit did not reach `main`.
- If history was rewritten, put the SHA from before the rewrite on the first line of the reply.
- Never skip hooks (`--no-verify`) or signing unless the owner orders it.
